Re-reviewed exact head `5f141ce4b5672cc5a3a6dcd764764d52f095a854` against previous converged head `92269cb5e3f591128c4102240499af45056ed418` and pinned master `1da79492f95e9b70dc67b039d7eef1f97682876a`. Thanks @pxxD1998. The upstream merge needs two small corrections before another gate.

The previous parent/child menu ownership and touch-release findings remain resolved within the focused coverage. I am not reopening the Oct 3 RED. These findings are in the new manual placement/scheduling integration:

### 1. The no-visualViewport fallback skips horizontal placement

`static/sessions.js:10677–10695` returns after assigning top/bottom, before width caps and the common `left` clamp at 10744–10747. `_showProjectPicker` sets `right:auto`, so the picker is no longer horizontally attached to its anchor.

With an anchor whose right edge is x440, the production-function control places the picker at x260–440. The current head never sets `style.left`; both previous-head and pinned-master controls set it. A separate Chromium DOM probe with `visualViewport` deliberately disabled confirms candidate x8–176 versus both controls x272–440. This tests fallback behavior, not a claim that Chromium ordinarily lacks that API.

**Fix:** apply common width sizing before either branch measures height, and run the measured-width horizontal clamp/left assignment in both branches. Keep the upstream vertical slide/tall-menu behavior and keyboard hooks. Add a no-visualViewport behavioral assertion for horizontal anchor ownership and narrow-window containment; the current fallback test asserts only vertical geometry.

### 2. A queued viewport update discards a subsequent scroll-close requirement

`static/sessions.js:10577–10588`: `scheduleReposition(false)` queues a callback, then `scheduleReposition(true)` returns because a frame is already queued. The callback retains false, so a row scrolled completely out of the list still owns an open picker.

Reproduction: list clip y200–650; open with anchor y400–440; move anchor to y150–190; dispatch visual-viewport resize, then list scroll, then flush one frame. Candidate keeps the picker mounted at y194–314 with an observer and two document listeners. The previous head removes it and releases those resources. Reversing the two events on the candidate also removes it.

**Fix:** OR each incoming close requirement into pending state before the queued-frame early return; consume/reset that state in the callback, and clear it during teardown. Test both event orders, one-frame coalescing, actual removal and listener/observer release.

The cumulative exact-head threat scan was CLEAN. The five submitted picker/menu/touch/pin/keyboard files passed **105 tests** (and 105 again after probe cleanup). Independent fail-first production-function probes, replayed by the parent reviewer, returned **2 candidate failures / 9 passing controls and diagnostic cases**; a separate Chromium probe confirmed finding 1. In-memory fix hypotheses restored control parity, but no contributor code was edited. These are sandboxed function/DOM results, not populated full-app screenshot acceptance or a full release certification.

Separately, an inherited context-point tracking residual exists on both previous and current heads: after a partially visible fork row moves up 100px, the saved client point keeps the picker 104px below the row. This is not introduced by this merge. A follow-up test should cover partial scrolling as well as full exit and ownership through repaint.

Please repair the two merge-introduced cases and re-push. The authoritative current-head gate and final visual approval still remain after those repairs.

